messages.say:
You are performing the final pre-experiment readiness audit of Babel, an autonomous coding-agent harness.

The question is no longer whether Babel has enough features.

The question is:

«If a capable coding/reasoning model works through Babel Chat today and succeeds, stalls, degrades, or fails, can we trust that behavior as meaningful evidence about the model rather than an artifact of Babel's Chat engine, TUI, headless mode, context management, tools, provider transport, policies, budgets, persistence, or surrounding infrastructure?»

Inspect the current live repository and current "main". Do not rely on the summaries below when the implementation can be inspected directly.

This is a final readiness pass before controlled model testing. You have authority to recommend delaying the benchmark campaign if Chat itself is not yet epistemically trustworthy.

Do not optimize for making Babel look ready.

Do not invent another architecture generation unless a concrete defect requires one.

Current context

Babel has recently completed a large pretest integration campaign.

Recent work on "main" includes, among other things:

V3 controller-verifiable independent-agent review authority;

canonical Babel Chat dogfooding/reviewer work;

provider/tool-cycle wire-protocol validation;

compaction provider/model safety;

explicit long-task wall profiles;

unit-test ambient-inference guards;

truthful daemon evidence;

M/B/I experiment contracts;

Run Intelligence canonical receipt admission;

lifecycle/TUI observation;

centralized worktree infrastructure;

I01 observe-only intervention support.

A final readiness report may exist as:

"ASTRA_PRETEST_READINESS_FINAL.md"

Treat it as a claim to audit, not authoritative truth.

Several known caveats have recently included things such as:

provider protocol enforcement potentially differing between development/CI and production;

unit inference guards covering "globalThis.fetch" but not every possible transport;

long-task wall authorization being separate from cost ceilings;

operator-defined compaction endpoints retaining some legacy behavior;

some infrastructure tests not necessarily wired into every CI lane.

Verify the current implementation rather than assuming those limitations remain exactly as described.

Objective

Perform a deep end-to-end audit of everything involved in Babel Chat.

The audit should answer:

«Is Babel Chat ready to serve as the primary execution harness for a serious autonomous-agent capability experiment?»

And more specifically:

«What, if anything, could currently stop, constrain, confuse, mislead, prematurely terminate, degrade, or falsely judge an otherwise capable agent?»

I care much more about finding hidden constraints than about proving the existing implementation correct.

Audit the complete Chat stack

Trace actual execution from the user's invocation all the way through model requests, tool use, persistence, recovery, and completion.

Do not inspect modules in isolation without reconstructing their effective runtime behavior.

Interactive TUI Chat

Determine exactly what happens when a human opens Babel and uses normal interactive Chat through the TUI.

Trace:

CLI/TUI entrypoint;

session creation;

ChatEngine creation;

configuration resolution;

execution profile;

model/provider routing;

model fallback;

system prompt construction;

project instructions;

memory/context loading;

tool registration;

permissions;

tool approval behavior;

shell execution;

file reads/writes;

test execution;

MCP;

subagents/delegation;

streaming;

rendering;

cancellation;

interruption;

continuation;

compaction;

persistence;

resume;

terminal decisions;

budget/policy decisions;

lifecycle telemetry;

failure presentation to the user.

Identify every place where the TUI can influence the underlying agent beyond presentation.

The important distinction is:

«Does TUI Chat run the same underlying agent with a UI around it, or does the TUI materially change capability, context, lifecycle, permissions, timing, or state?»

Normal Chat mode

Audit canonical ChatEngine behavior independently of UI concerns.

Reconstruct its effective state machine.

I want to understand:

how a task begins;

what the model sees on turn 1;

how context evolves;

how model-visible history differs from durable history;

how tool calls become durable events;

how results return to the model;

how retries affect history;

how provider fallbacks affect history;

how compaction rewrites or summarizes context;

how long sessions remain coherent;

what counts as progress;

what counts as a stall;

what counts as mutation;

when nudges occur;

when hard caps occur;

when policies intervene;

when the engine terminates;

how terminal outcomes are classified.

Look particularly for interactions between independently reasonable components that can combine into pathological behavior.

Headless Chat

Trace the real headless execution path used for autonomous/non-interactive work.

Do not assume it behaves like interactive Chat merely because both eventually instantiate "ChatEngine".

Compare headless vs TUI Chat across:

engine options;

execution profile;

environment variables;

task class;

system prompt;

model policy;

provider routing;

tools;

permissions;

approval semantics;

memory;

compaction;

budgets;

wall limits;

stall thresholds;

stdout/output contracts;

process lifetime;

signal handling;

cancellation;

recovery;

persistence;

subagent behavior;

telemetry.

Produce an explicit:

TUI CHAT vs HEADLESS CHAT

behavioral-difference table.

Classify every difference as:

intentional presentation/interface behavior;

necessary automation behavior;

harmless implementation detail;

experimental contaminant;

likely bug.

The key question:

«If the same model receives the same coding task in TUI Chat and headless Chat, should we expect materially the same capability?»

If not, explain exactly why.

Subagents and delegated execution

Inspect how Chat spawns or interacts with subagents.

Determine:

what context they receive;

what context they do not receive;

tool differences;

provider/model differences;

workspace isolation;

environment inheritance;

budget inheritance;

wall-time inheritance;

cost inheritance;

cancellation propagation;

error propagation;

result truncation;

result reintegration;

whether the parent can distinguish subagent failure from task failure.

Investigate whether delegation can accidentally make a strong parent agent weaker.

Provider boundary

Audit the exact path from model-neutral conversation state to provider-specific API request.

Inspect:

route resolution;

provider selection;

fallback selection;

model identity;

endpoint selection;

credential selection;

request serialization;

tool definitions;

tool-call IDs;

tool-result IDs;

parallel tools;

partial tool batches;

failed tool calls;

structured output;

reasoning/thinking modes;

token limits;

streaming termination;

malformed streams;

retries;

provider errors;

timeout behavior.

Pay special attention to the recently added provider protocol invariant.

Ask:

«Can malformed model-visible history still leave the process under any configuration?»

And:

«Is the invariant enforced in the actual experiment configuration, or merely observed?»

Do not treat shadow-mode detection as equivalent to prevention.

Context and compaction

This is a major focus.

A capable agent can appear incapable if the harness destroys or distorts its context.

Audit:

context-window accounting;

token estimation;

prompt growth;

tool-result growth;

large file/tool outputs;

conversation persistence;

summary generation;

compaction triggers;

compaction model/provider routing;

heuristic compaction fallback;

summary fidelity;

lost requirements;

duplicated context;

stale context;

resume after compaction;

repeated compaction;

interaction with provider tool protocol;

interaction with subagents;

interaction with long tasks.

Construct realistic failure scenarios.

Examples:

100+ tool calls;

multiple edits and test cycles;

large compiler/test output;

repeated failed attempts;

one or more compactions;

provider retry;

resumed session;

delegated subtask;

final verification.

Ask whether the model still has enough correct information to finish.

Tools

Audit the tools the model actually sees and can use.

Look at:

file reads;

exact-range reads;

grep/search;

semantic search if present;

file writes;

replacements;

shell;

tests;

Git;

worktrees;

MCP;

memory;

subagents.

Check for:

incorrect schemas;

confusing descriptions;

hidden restrictions;

path problems;

output truncation;

timeout mismatches;

excessive output;

silent errors;

platform differences;

tools reporting success when the requested effect did not occur.

Pay special attention to whether the model has enough power to recover from its own mistakes.

Windows vs Linux

Babel is regularly used on Windows but may also run on Linux/cloud machines.

Identify Chat behavior that differs materially across:

path semantics;

quoting;

shells;

executable discovery;

permissions;

process trees;

signals;

timeout/kill behavior;

Git;

worktrees;

temporary directories;

line endings;

environment inheritance.

A benchmark should not accidentally measure Windows subprocess pathology as model capability.

Long-running autonomy

Audit Babel specifically for tasks lasting approximately:

30 minutes;

1 hour;

2 hours;

4 hours.

Trace all independent clocks and limits.

Include:

Chat wall;

process wall;

child/subagent wall;

provider timeout;

tool timeout;

shell timeout;

stall detection;

post-write/anti-thrash windows;

critic/repair windows;

cost budget;

token budget;

retry budget;

max turns.

Construct a timeline showing which component would terminate first under representative long-running behavior.

Look for cases where Babel advertises a 4-hour task but some deeper layer still kills it after 10, 30, or 60 minutes.

Policies and anti-thrash mechanisms

Audit every mechanism intended to stop unproductive agents.

This includes concepts such as:

investigate hard caps;

tools-without-write counters;

repeated-read detection;

mutation expectations;

stall detection;

forced intervention;

nudges;

budget kills;

post-write windows;

critic escalation;

terminal arbitration.

For each mechanism determine:

what behavior triggers it;

whether capable models could legitimately exhibit that behavior;

whether it changes model behavior;

whether it can terminate otherwise recoverable work;

whether its activation is observable in evidence;

whether the experiment can distinguish:
MODEL_STALL

HARNESS_POLICY_INTERVENTION

TOOL_FAILURE

CONTEXT_FAILURE.

The I01 experiment exists precisely because a harness policy may masquerade as a model failure.

Search for additional I01-like candidates.

Persistence and resume

Simulate or reason through:

clean session;

interrupted process;

restarted CLI;

resumed run;

provider retry;

partially completed tool cycle;

process killed during tool execution;

compaction immediately before shutdown;

session restored after long inactivity.

Determine whether resumed execution is behaviorally equivalent enough to uninterrupted execution.

Look for:

lost tool results;

duplicate results;

replay;

corrupted state;

stale model-visible state;

state the model cannot reconcile.

Terminal outcomes and evidence

Audit how Chat decides and reports:

success;

no change required;

blocked policy;

blocked permission;

timeout;

budget exhaustion;

provider failure;

tool failure;

cancellation;

model completion;

unknown/inconclusive state.

Ask:

«Could a harness-generated terminal outcome currently be mistaken for model inability?»

And:

«Could a model-generated success claim currently be mistaken for task correctness?»

The deterministic verifier should remain the correctness authority where appropriate.

TUI observability

Evaluate whether the TUI gives the operator enough information to diagnose an autonomous run without changing it.

At minimum examine whether the operator can understand:

what model is active;

whether fallback occurred;

current task/phase;

current tool;

progress;

token/context pressure;

compaction;

elapsed time;

effective wall;

budget;

policy interventions;

retries;

stalls;

recovery;

subagent activity;

terminal reason.

Do not require every metric to be visually displayed if evidence is stored elsewhere.

But identify UX deficiencies likely to cause operators to interrupt healthy agents because Babel appears frozen.

Controlled tests / canaries

Do not immediately launch an expensive benchmark campaign.

Design and, where practical, run the smallest deterministic or mock-provider tests needed to validate your conclusions.

Prefer:

fake provider;

scripted model responses;

deterministic fixture;

controlled tool failures;

controlled compaction;

controlled retries;

controlled resumes;

controlled timeout conditions.

You may propose a very small real-model canary if something cannot be tested meaningfully otherwise.

Do not burn large amounts of inference simply to claim realism.

Cross-mode parity tests

I specifically want tests capable of detecting unintended drift between:

TUI Chat

headless Chat

canonical ChatEngine

Where possible, compare the resolved runtime state, not self-declared helper objects.

For the same controlled task compare:

system context;

provider policy;

model;

tool schemas;

execution profile;

budgets;

limits;

context/compaction;

permissions;

terminal behavior.

I want confidence that changing interface does not secretly change measured capability.

Failure attribution matrix

Build a causal failure taxonomy.

At minimum distinguish:

MODEL_REASONING_FAILURE

MODEL_TOOL_SELECTION_FAILURE

MODEL_STALL

CONTEXT_LOSS

COMPACTION_ERROR

RETRIEVAL_FAILURE

TOOL_FAILURE

TOOL_PROTOCOL_FAILURE

PROVIDER_FAILURE

PROVIDER_RETRY_ARTIFACT

PERMISSION_BLOCK

HARNESS_POLICY_BLOCK

BUDGET_LIMIT

WALL_TIMEOUT

SUBAGENT_FAILURE

WORKSPACE/GIT_FAILURE

VERIFIER_FAILURE

ENVIRONMENT_FAILURE

INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE

OPERATOR_INTERRUPTION

UNKNOWN

For each, identify which current evidence would let us classify it.

Flag categories Babel cannot yet reliably distinguish.

Adversarial requirement

Before concluding Babel Chat is ready, construct the strongest argument that the current system could still generate misleading capability results.

Assume:

the model is capable;

CI is green;

unit tests are correct;

ordinary short Chat tasks work.

Find ways a serious autonomous run could nevertheless fail because of Babel.

Do not dismiss low-frequency issues merely because they are hard to reproduce if they would invalidate a benchmark conclusion.

Then distinguish:

credible risk;

theoretical risk;

already mitigated risk.

Evidence standard

Separate throughout:

DIRECTLY OBSERVED

Behavior demonstrated in current code/tests/runtime.

EXTERNALLY SUPPORTED

Relevant provider/platform/documentation evidence.

EVIDENCE-BACKED INFERENCE

Strong conclusions derived from observed implementation.

HYPOTHESIS

Needs testing.

SPECULATION

Plausible but weakly supported.

Do not mix these categories.

Falsification

For every major conclusion, state what evidence would prove it wrong.

Examples:

If you conclude TUI/headless parity is good:

«What test result would falsify that?»

If you conclude compaction is safe enough:

«What realistic workload would disprove it?»

If you conclude long-task mode is functional:

«What deeper timeout would reverse that conclusion?»

I want conclusions that are testable rather than confidence statements.

Prioritization rule

Do not generate a long wishlist.

Classify discovered issues as:

P0 — BENCHMARK INVALIDATING

Could cause model capability to be mismeasured or misattributed.

P1 — SERIOUS EXPERIMENTAL RISK

Could contaminate results but is detectable/recoverable.

P2 — PRODUCT QUALITY

Worth fixing but does not block controlled experiments.

P3 — POLISH

Defer.

Only P0/P1 should normally delay testing.

Avoid architecture churn

The default action for a defect is:

«smallest change that restores truthful measurement.»

Do not respond to every weakness by creating:

another governance version;

another reviewer framework;

another evidence schema;

another benchmark system;

another agent mode.

Reuse existing systems unless evidence proves them insufficient.

Final decision

Finish with exactly one of:

RUN

Babel Chat is sufficiently trustworthy for the controlled benchmark campaign.

RUN AFTER SPECIFIC FIXES

The architecture is fundamentally ready, but identified P0/P1 fixes must land first.

DO NOT RUN YET

The Chat stack still has a structural problem that prevents trustworthy capability measurement.

Required final deliverable

Produce:

Decision

RUN / RUN AFTER SPECIFIC FIXES / DO NOT RUN YET

Executive finding

Can Babel Chat currently serve as a trustworthy autonomous-agent harness?

End-to-end Chat architecture

Trace:

user/TUI or headless invocation
→ ChatEngine
→ context
→ routing
→ provider
→ tool loop
→ persistence
→ compaction
→ policy/budget
→ verifier/evidence
→ terminal outcome

TUI vs headless parity matrix

Agent capability bottlenecks

Anything Babel currently does that could hold back a capable model.

Long-task termination analysis

All clocks/limits and which one actually dominates.

Context/compaction analysis

Provider/tool protocol analysis

Policy-intervention analysis

Including additional I01-like candidates if any.

Failure-attribution matrix

P0/P1 findings

Exact files/functions/runtime paths and reproducible evidence.

Minimum fixes

Only work necessary before tests.

Pretest canary plan

Smallest validation necessary after fixes.

Strongest case your conclusion is wrong

Falsification criteria

Remaining uncertainty most likely to reverse the decision

Completion condition

You are done only when you can answer:

«If a capable agent fails during the next Babel experiment, are we now in a position to determine whether Babel itself caused or materially contributed to that failure?»

Do not begin the full benchmark campaign.

Audit first.

If the correct answer is RUN, tell us exactly what the first controlled experiment should be.

If the correct answer is RUN AFTER SPECIFIC FIXES, define the smallest implementation package and the exact tests that would move the decision to RUN.

If the correct answer is DO NOT RUN YET, identify the narrow structural reason—not a generic list of improvements.
